{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A3QiY21act4MKbSXKaveiB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd012-yes\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/yes: Repeatedly Output a String\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Scripts and automation tools use yes to feed affirmative input to interactive programs\n6\t  or to generate large volumes of repeated output for benchmarking. The macOS BSD yes\n7\t  differs from GNU yes in argument handling and output buffering. The Go implementation\n8\t  must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gyes, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte\n9\t  on all argument combinations and write-error behavior, verified by differential testing\n10\t  via pkg/testutils.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Output a string repeatedly until killed or a write error occurs, matching GNU yes behavior.\n14\t  - G2: Support zero or more string arguments, joining them with spaces when multiple are given.\n15\t  - G3: Achieve high throughput by buffering output, avoiding per-line syscall overhead.\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Core output behavior\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: When no arguments are given, must output \"y\" followed by a newline, repeatedly, until the process is killed or a write error occurs on stdout.\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: When one or more arguments are given, must join them with a single space character and output the resulting string followed by a newline, repeatedly.\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: Arguments beginning with \"-\" must be passable using the \"--\" separator to prevent misinterpretation as flags (e.g., \"yes -- --help\" outputs \"--help\" repeatedly).\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: Must not read from stdin. All output goes to stdout.\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Output buffering and performance\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: Must buffer output to avoid per-line write syscalls. Use a bufio.Writer with a buffer of at least 8192 bytes or write in fixed-size blocks, matching the GNU implementation's approach of filling a large output buffer before calling write().\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: Must flush the output buffer before exiting on a write error so that the last partial buffer is delivered to the pipe reader.\n37\t\n38\t  R3:\n39\t    title: Exit codes and signal handling\n40\t    items:\n41\t      - R3.1:\n42\t          text: Must exit 0 when terminated by SIGPIPE (the normal case when piped to a program that closes its stdin).\n43\t      - R3.2:\n44\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error other than EPIPE occurs on stdout.\n45\t      - R3.3:\n46\t          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully without printing an error message to stderr. The default Go SIGPIPE behavior (exit silently) is acceptable if it matches the reference binary's exit code.\n47\t\n48\t  R4:\n49\t    title: Differential testing\n50\t    items:\n51\t      - R4.1:\n52\t          text: Differential tests must pipe the output of both the Go binary and the reference binary through \"head -n N\" and compare the captured output byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.\n53\t      - R4.2:\n54\t          text: \"Tests must cover: no arguments (default 'y'), single argument, multiple arguments joined with spaces, arguments after '--' separator, and empty string argument.\"\n55\t      - R4.3:\n56\t          text: Tests must verify exit code behavior when stdout is closed early (SIGPIPE case).\n57\t\n58\tnon_goals:\n59\t  - cmd/yes does not implement any flags beyond --help and --version.\n60\t  - cmd/yes does not read from stdin or accept input from any source other than command-line arguments.\n61\t  - cmd/yes does not implement a line-count limit; it runs until killed or a write error occurs.\n62\t\n63\tacceptance_criteria:\n64\t  - id: AC1\n65\t    criterion: \"yes | head -n 5 produces five lines of 'y', matching gyes | head -n 5 byte-for-byte.\"\n66\t    traces:\n67\t      - R4.1\n68\t  - id: AC2\n69\t    criterion: \"yes hello world | head -n 3 produces three lines of 'hello world', matching gyes.\"\n70\t    traces:\n71\t      - R4.1\n72\t  - id: AC3\n73\t    criterion: \"yes -- --help | head -n 1 produces '--help', matching gyes.\"\n74\t    traces:\n75\t      - R1.3\n76\t      - R4.1\n77\t  - id: AC4\n78\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gyes passes for default, single-arg, multi-arg, and '--' separator cases.\"\n79\t    traces:\n80\t      - R4.1\n81\t  - id: AC5\n82\t    criterion: \"cmd/yes compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n83\t    traces:\n84\t      - R1.1\n85\t      - R1.2\n86\t      - R1.3\n87\t      - R1.4\n88\t      - R2.1\n89\t      - R2.2\n90\t      - R3.1\n91\t      - R3.2\n92\t      - R3.3\n93\t      - R4.1\n94\t      - R4.2\n95\t      - R4.3\n96\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed0e1932-750d-44c2-9378-90254e3e2f4c","uuid":"dd28e5f8-7313-41c2-a4b4-31ab61d88b44","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:56:22.903Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5143/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd012-yes.yaml","content":"id: srd012-yes\ntitle: \"cmd/yes: Repeatedly Output a String\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Scripts and automation tools use yes to feed affirmative input to interactive programs\n  or to generate large volumes of repeated output for benchmarking. The macOS BSD yes\n  differs from GNU yes in argument handling and output buffering. The Go implementation\n  must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gyes, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte\n  on all argument combinations and write-error behavior, verified by differential testing\n  via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Output a string repeatedly until killed or a write error occurs, matching GNU yes behavior.\n  - G2: Support zero or more string arguments, joining them with spaces when multiple are given.\n  - G3: Achieve high throughput by buffering output, avoiding per-line syscall overhead.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Core output behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: When no arguments are given, must output \"y\" followed by a newline, repeatedly, until the process is killed or a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: When one or more arguments are given, must join them with a single space character and output the resulting string followed by a newline, repeatedly.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: Arguments beginning with \"-\" must be passable using the \"--\" separator to prevent misinterpretation as flags (e.g., \"yes -- --help\" outputs \"--help\" repeatedly).\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must not read from stdin. All output goes to stdout.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Output buffering and performance\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: Must buffer output to avoid per-line write syscalls. Use a bufio.Writer with a buffer of at least 8192 bytes or write in fixed-size blocks, matching the GNU implementation's approach of filling a large output buffer before calling write().\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must flush the output buffer before exiting on a write error so that the last partial buffer is delivered to the pipe reader.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Exit codes and signal handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when terminated by SIGPIPE (the normal case when piped to a program that closes its stdin).\n      - R3.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a write error other than EPIPE occurs on stdout.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully without printing an error message to stderr. The default Go SIGPIPE behavior (exit silently) is acceptable if it matches the reference binary's exit code.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Differential tests must pipe the output of both the Go binary and the reference binary through \"head -n N\" and compare the captured output byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: \"Tests must cover: no arguments (default 'y'), single argument, multiple arguments joined with spaces, arguments after '--' separator, and empty string argument.\"\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Tests must verify exit code behavior when stdout is closed early (SIGPIPE case).\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/yes does not implement any flags beyond --help and --version.\n  - cmd/yes does not read from stdin or accept input from any source other than command-line arguments.\n  - cmd/yes does not implement a line-count limit; it runs until killed or a write error occurs.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"yes | head -n 5 produces five lines of 'y', matching gyes | head -n 5 byte-for-byte.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.1\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"yes hello world | head -n 3 produces three lines of 'hello world', matching gyes.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.1\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"yes -- --help | head -n 1 produces '--help', matching gyes.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.3\n      - R4.1\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gyes passes for default, single-arg, multi-arg, and '--' separator cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.1\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"cmd/yes compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n","numLines":96,"startLine":1,"totalLines":96}}}
