tion...\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the refactoring approach—breaking main into separate functions like `handleFlags` and `writeLoop` keeps things organized, but the current main is already compact and the 40-line limit is strict. I should probably leave it as is.\n\nOn the --help and --version flags, the task title mentions them and the requirements reference feature-completeness, so I think they're in scope despite not being explicitly listed. I'll add a minimal implementation.\n\nBut I need to verify whether extracting `handleFlags` would cause any test failures before committing to that structure. If I add `--version` handling, and a test runs `yes --version`, it would exit immediately instead of outputting \"--version\" repeatedly. But the existing tests don't test this case, so it should be fine.\n\nWait, what about `yes -- --version`? The existing test has `dash-dash-arg` which tests `-- --help`. With the flag handling in place, `--` would prevent `--help` from being treated as a flag, and it would output \"--help\" repeatedly. That's correct because the existing `handleFlags` would check if `args[0] == \"--\"` and return false.\n\nFor the test updates, I need to add stderr capture to the SIGPIPE test for R3.3, while the existing TestDiff already covers R4.1 and R4.2.\n\nLooking at the existing main function more carefully, it's only about 12 lines, so I can add the --help and --version flags inline without extracting separate functions. I'm adding the flag checks before the main output loop, handling both cases and returning early if matched.\n\nWriting the implementation now... I'm modifying the test to compare the Go binary's behavior against the reference `gyes` binary, capturing both exit codes and stderr to ensure they match. I'll also need to update the helper function to return stderr alongside the exit code.\n\nWriting test helper function... I see that `errors.AsType` is already used in the existing test code at line 85, so that approach will work. I'm